/*
  Localizable.strings (District AI)

  ⛔ THE ONLY THING IN HERE IS THE COPY APNs DRAWS A NOTIFICATION FROM, AND IT IS
  NOT AN INVITATION TO START LOCALISING THE APP. Every other string in this client
  is an English `Text("…")` literal in its view, by decision; these six exist
  because the SERVER cannot send the words. The server builds `aps.alert` as
  `title-loc-key` / `loc-key`, which are KEYS into this table, resolved by the OS on the handset without waking the app.

  ⛔ SO THESE SIX SENTENCES ARE READ OFF A LOCKED PHONE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
  REASON THEY NAME NOBODY. The push payload carries ids and nothing else (no
  message body, no caller, no number), precisely so a notification cannot disclose
  who is contacting whom to whoever is holding the device. Copy that said "New
  message from Jane" would have to come from the server and would undo that on
  every phone at once. Whatever detail is wanted belongs behind the tap, where the
  app has a bearer and knows who is asking.

  ⚠️ A KEY REMOVED HERE DOES NOT FAIL, IT SHOWS THE KEY. An unresolved
  `title-loc-key` renders as the literal string `push.message.title` on the lock
  screen. So this file and the server's key list move together, and the generic pair is
  the server's fallback for a push type it has not been taught, and it must exist here
  even though nothing sends it today.
*/

"push.message.title" = "New message";
"push.message.body" = "A new message arrived in your inbox.";

"push.call.title" = "Incoming call";
"push.call.body" = "Someone is calling your District line.";

"push.generic.title" = "District AI";
"push.generic.body" = "You have a new notification.";
